Here's what it looked like... 
Troubleshooting: Lambda "AccessDeniedException" on DynamoDB

My visitor counter Lambda returned an AccessDeniedException on UpdateItem, with a message saying no identity-based policy allowed the action. I worked through the IAM side first. I corrected the policy action from UpdateTable to UpdateItem, confirmed the policy was attached to the right execution role, verified the table ARN, and used the IAM policy simulator to confirm the role was allowed to perform UpdateItem.

The root cause was in my code, not IAM. DynamoDB table names are case sensitive, and my function referenced "counter" while the table is named "Counter." Fixing the capitalization resolved it. Takeaway: an access denied error can be a name mismatch in disguise, and the policy simulator is a fast way to rule IAM in or out. Who knew!? Well now it's me, I know now.

